        <body>
            <div xml:id="d159">

                <pb facs="0341" n="241" sameAs="frus:frus1880/pg241"/>

                <head>No. 159.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Seward to Mr. Evarts.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1880-03-27"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 27, 1880</date>.
                        (Received June 8.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 643.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Recurring to my dispatch of the 16th
                    instant, No. 623, in regard to a proposed tax on cargo boats (lighters), I have
                    now the honor to hand to you a letter which has been addressed to the British
                    minister, as doyen of, the diplomatic body, by the Tsung-li Yamên, in reference
                    to the inquiries made by the Chinese secretary of his legation. You will see
                    that the ministers, while upholding their right to tax cargo-boats, are willing
                    to waive such taxation, and indicate that rules for the registration of such
                    craft are needed in order to prevent smuggling, and will be framed and
                    communicated to the foreign representatives.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>GEORGE F. SEWABD.</signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d159-a1.xml" xpointer="d159-a1"/>
            </div>
